    <p>
        I wanted to make a generic LinkedIn post about my twelve months here at
        MDA Space. I wanted to talk about how fun the stuff I did was, how
        fantastic the social aspects of it were, how interesting it was for me,
        someone who isn't in engineering to work in aerospace, talk about being
        grateful, blah, blah, blah. All of that is true, but the actual truth is
        much more complicated that it ever needed to be. There were social
        events. There was being left behind. There was mentorship. There was
        miscommunication. There were interesting technologies. There were broken
        boards (six of them). There was closeness. There was significant
        rupture. There was even a point where I came frighteningly close to
        there never being a Kershan Arulneswaran roaming around that office,
        ever again. Despite all of that, I pulled through. Not everything went
        back to the way it was before. Honestly, neither did I, far from it. But
        somewhere in all of that sweet chaos, I learned a ridiculous amount
        about engineering, about people, about myself. I made mistakes, some
        minor ones, some that accidentally pushed away the people I loved the
        most. I made friends. I built some cool shit. I broke some too. Somehow,
        when I look back at the entire year instead of just its worst moments,
        I'm still grateful I got to live it. And if I could go back in time? I'd
        fucking do it all over again.
    </p>

    <h2>You Probably Need Some Context</h2>
    <p>
        I feel like many people here don't know who I am, so allow me to
        introduce myself. Hi, I'm Kersh. I'm a Computer Science student at
        Ontario Tech University, a Canadian University you've probably never
        heard of, located in bumfuck nowhere, Ontario. Not in engineering by the
        way, Computer Science, because Grade 12 me thought that Computer Science
        and Computer Engineering were like 95% identical. Well, now I know.
    </p>
    <p>
        Despite this, I worked with a robotics design team for three years now
        as a programmer, lead and mentor, doing Robot in 3 Days and the ARC
        (formerly RoboMaster NA) competition. I tinker with electronics as a
        hobby, and spent a lot of time with computer vision and artificial
        intelligence. Before MDA, I worked as a teaching assistant for a Java
        application course and a co-op at a private school where I was a program
        manager and facilitator for STEM summer education programs. Growing up,
        I've always liked the combination of software and hardware, but this is
        my first time actually working on this as a paid role. I was about to
        learn very quickly that this is a completely different ballpark from
        application development.
    </p>
    <h3>So, Why MDA?</h3>
    <p>
        June 15, 2025. I was sitting in my room, bored while building an
        admittedly very easy Gunpla. My first co-op as a program manager intern
        building content had been fun. But, it was only four months long, I was
        missing that stability and continuity, and that schedule worked a hell
        of a lot better for Waterloo students, like my fellow intern who went
        there, than it did for someone at Ontario Tech. I needed something else.
    </p>
    <p>
        More importantly, I wanted something technical. I wanted to work in a
        field I was actually interested in, so I started casually scrolling
        through LinkedIn for internships starting in the fall. Eventually, I
        found a posting from MDA looking for a software intern. My thought
        process was basically: “Hey, I'm a software student. I worked in
        robotics. Robots are cool. This company seems cool. Looking at all of
        the interns there, they seem cool. Eh, this could be interesting.” So I
        applied.
    </p>
    <p>
        I already knew MDA as the company behind Canadarm and Canadarm2, and I
        knew they did a bunch of work with satellites too. But unlike a lot of
        people who probably apply because they love aerospace, it was the
        “Robotics” part of “Robotics and Space Operations” that hooked me in. I
        wanted software that had to interact with actual hardware, and MDA
        looked like a pretty damn good place to find it.
    </p>
    <p>
        I was still curious about aerospace, though. Part of why I wanted
        internships in the first place was to see all the weird places a
        Computer Science degree could take me, and apparently space was one of
        them. So I figured I'd blend in with the crowd. Go undercover. Become
        James Bond. Learn what the hell everyone was talking about. And, like
        probably 80% of people, I wanted to be an astronaut when I was a little
        kid. By taking a job in the space industry, I wanted to see how the
        industry responsible for things like that actually came together. How
        Artemis II came together. How the ISS came together. How the Canadarm
        came together.
    </p>
    <p>
        Shit, it's slowly hitting me now, after a year, that I'm contributing to
        the kind of thing that would've blown that kid's mind. It's also
        objectively funny that I, a Computer Science student who originally
        applied because “robot gud,” somehow ended up working on critical ground
        and flight software. I don't know whether I should be proud, terrified,
        or just fully embrace the aerospace larp. Fuck it. I'll embrace it.
    </p>
    <h3>Obviously, I Can't Tell You Everything</h3>
    <p>
        MDA is an aerospace company, which means a lot of the work happens
        behind closed doors. I can talk about the surface-level stuff I worked
        on, but I won't be sharing internal details about projects, tools,
        systems, or anything else that isn't mine to share. This also isn't
        meant to tell future interns or employees what their experience at MDA
        will be like. I'm only writing about my experience, what I took away
        from it, and the lessons I learned along the way. Treat everything here
        as one person's perspective, not a definitive review of the company.
        Finally, parts of this story get pretty dark. Sensitive acts will have a
        trigger warning before them, so please read those sections at your own
        discretion.
    </p>
    <h2>Act I — Tested My Fate</h2>
    <p>
        After an interview, a signed offer, a ton of security paperwork, and a
        few weeks of waiting, I finally stepped foot in Brampton, ready to start
        my first day. I was kinda nervous, especially since I still have
        thoughts about my last co-op which ended a few days before. There was
        even someone I actually knew who's also starting (context, we met at a
        hackathon) that day. We did some onboarding, did a quick tour, and
        waited until I was assigned to my team. When I got called, my onboarding
        buddy told me that I was working on Canadarm2 ground software, but
        little did I know when I set foot on that floor that something was about
        to happen.
    </p>
    <p>
        I was lost. LOST. I was wondering what everyone was talking about, what
        the hell I was supposed to do, what "SSRMS" even means, what software
        toolchain was used, why everyone was on vacation on my first day and why
        all of these acronyms make ZERO sense. Fortunately, there were two
        mechanical interns that did introduce me and another intern to some
        resources, what this program actually is for the company, our
        partnership with the Canadian Space Agency, and also introduced us to
        the wider intern network. I also found out that I, an Ontario Tech
        student, actually replaced an intern that was supposed to be from
        Waterloo. Waterloo interns on that specific team were the rule and I was
        the exception. Admittedly though, the introduction of the wider network
        ultimately made me more lost than ever.
    </p>
    <p>
        The thing is, I was at a pretty big disadvantage socially. Obviously,
        I'm not in Engineering. I would be in Computer Engineering, but thank my
        uni for not offering it. That's besides the point though. I was in a
        department that rarely hires interns longer than four months, I came
        from a school that definitely wasn't part of the usual pipeline, was the
        only intern on my team, and that entire network mostly consisting of
        either Mechanical or Aerospace majors. I was also on a different floor,
        was always sat with full-timers instead of with other interns, doing a
        fucking HONOURS THESIS WHILE ON CO-OP because I hate free time which was
        a bad idea in hindsight, and many of the interns there already formed
        tight social groups. This was significantly different from just me and a
        Waterloo student at the last one. I needed to make a choice. Either I
        could put my head down, do my job, and spend twelve months mostly by
        myself, or I could deliberately put myself out there and try to become
        part of a group of people I otherwise probably never would've met. For a
        while, I was kinda toying with either option, but ultimately I wanted to
        live this shit to the fullest in the twelve months I thought I had. And
        this decision was amplified by one thing: basketball.
    </p>
    <p>
        I love basketball. I'd say I'm pretty good at it. I love playing it, I
        love watching it, I love yapping about it to my friends, I even did a
        data analysis project to confirm that yes, centers are overpowered,
        noob. I'm a Raptors fan until the day I die, and I will 100 percent shit
        on Sixers fans who are still crying about 2019. You get the point.
        Imagine my surprise when I found out there was an MDA intern NBA fantasy
        league. I was glad that other people were into the same sports and
        everything related to it as I was, and I was also glad that we could be
        closer. Sure, it was just me, the same guy I met at a hackathon from
        before, and eight more people that I was somewhat tight with at the
        intern lunch table, but we did bond and became fast friends (in addition
        to some others who weren't in the league ofc). Obviously, I was still a
        little lost because of everything I had working against me socially but
        during that time I felt calm.
    </p>
