    <h2>Start with the useful part</h2>
    <p>
        A Kersh post should feel considered without feeling formal. Explain the
        problem in plain language, include the constraints that shaped the
        outcome, and keep every technical detail attached to a reason.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>State the problem and why it mattered.</li>
        <li>Show the decision that changed the direction.</li>
        <li>Leave the reader with something they can reuse.</li>
    </ul>

    <blockquote>
        The strongest detail is usually the one that explains why the obvious
        solution was not quite right.
    </blockquote>

    <h2>Show the work</h2>
    <p>
        Use a short example when the implementation matters. The layout supports
        inline <code>technical details</code>, lists, links, pull quotes, and
        focused code blocks without turning the post into documentation.
    </p>
    <pre><code>const lesson = &#123;
  constraint: 'what made this hard',
  choice: 'what you tried',
  result: 'what changed'
&#125;;</code></pre>

    <h2>End with what changed</h2>
    <p>
        Close on the lesson, not a recap. Name what you would keep, what you
        would change next time, and the question you are still thinking about.
        That gives the post somewhere to land—and the next one somewhere to
        begin.
    </p>
</KershPostLayout>
